ogram for facial recognition in law enforcement and for algorithmic lending decisions, regardless of claimed benefits, until those systems demonstrate zero systematic disparate impact by race or gender over statistically significant samples. The measurable threshold is not 'better than human performance' or 'acceptable error rates'—it is the same standard we apply to human decision-makers under civil rights law: no systematic discrimination. If a human loan officer systematically denied applications from qualified Black borrowers, we would not allow them to pay higher fees to continue discriminating while working toward improvement. We would prohibit the discriminatory practice and impose liability for past harms. The compensation fund creates algorithmic exceptionalism—a two-tier civil rights regime where AI systems receive gentler treatment than human actors for identical discriminatory outcomes.\n\nThis argument assumes that current AI discrimination is primarily a design choice rather than an inevitable byproduct of biased training data beyond developer control. If training data bias were truly insurmountable—if no algorithmic design could overcome historical patterns in the data—then strict liability might indeed eliminate AI from these domains entirely. But empirical evidence contradicts this: researchers have demonstrated multiple techniques for bias mitigation in algorithmic decision-making, from adversarial debiasing to fairness constraints in optimization. The fact that developers deploy systems without implementing these techniques reveals a choice to prioritize other objectives—predictive accuracy, computational efficiency, time to market—over civil rights compliance. Strict liability would force developers to internalize the costs of that choice. The compensation fund would let them externalize those costs to a fee structure while victims receive administrative payments instead of the prohibition of discriminatory practices that civil rights law demands